  "query_preview": "..\n\n- Critical Fire Weather Conditions across Northern Rockies, this\n  afternoon through tonight. \n\n- Choppy wave activity on area lakes this afternoon and\n  evening. \n\n- Relatively cool air mass in place tomorrow through early next \n  week, but summer warmth returns thereafter.\n\nWith the ridge of high pressure moving eastward, low elevations \nwill see temperatures rise into the 90s and 100s (F) and will \ncombine with low relative humidity and gusty winds through this\nevening. As a result, critical fire weather conditions will exist\ntoday and tonight, with widespread Red Flag Warnings issued \nacross the Northern Rockies. Please check for the latest local \nfire restrictions in place during this critical fire weather \nperiod. For those recreating on area lakes, particularly Flathead \nLake, expect choppy wave activity through late evening. \n\nA much cooler day is expected both Sunday and Monday, with highs \nonly reaching into the 70s. Sunday night into Monday, low \ntemperatures will drop into the 40s, and possibly even the high \n30s for high elevation valleys, including \u2013 but not limited to \u2013 \nareas like Dixie, ID. \n\nSome forecast models introduce scattered showers across northwest\nMontana Sunday night into Monday, mainly along the Canadian \nborder and into Glacier National Park. Given how dry the \natmosphere will be leading up to this, any shower activity may \ninitially bring gusty winds before rain actually falls. \n\nLong range ensembles generally agree that a warm high pressure\nridge will return to the Northern Rockies around the middle of\nnext week. Models also tend to agree that the next iteration of\nour summertime ridge will be several degrees cooler than the\ncurrent ridge.",
